  "final_response": "The Laplacian eigenvalues of $C_m$ are $2 - 2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi j}{m}$ for $0 \\le j < m$, and similarly for $C_n$. The Laplacian of $G = C_m \\square C_n$ is $L_G = L_{C_m} \\otimes I_n + I_m \\otimes L_{C_n}$, so its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_{j,k} = \\left(2 - 2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi j}{m}\\right) + \\left(2 - 2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi k}{n}\\right) = 4 - 2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi j}{m} - 2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi k}{n}$. The zero eigenvalue occurs only when $j=k=0$, corresponding to the constant eigenvector, and since $G$ is connected, this eigenvalue has multiplicity 1. By the Matrix Tree Theorem, $\\tau(G) = \\frac{1}{mn} \\prod_{\\substack{0\\le j<m,\\ 0\\le k<n\\\\(j,k)\\ne(0,0)}} \\lambda_{j,k} = \\frac{1}{mn} \\prod_{\\substack{0\\le j<m,\\ 0\\le k<n\\\\(j,k)\\ne(0,0)}} \\left(4 - 2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi j}{m} - 2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi k}{n}\\right)$.",
